AROS VNC server is based on libvncserver and uses some parts
of gemsvnc (framebuffer changes detection) 
and AmiVNC (keyboard and mouse inputs)

It can be launched directly from it's icon or from the shell.

When launched from shell the following options are available:
	HELP              Display this help message.
	
	STARTHIDDEN       Doesn't show GUI on startup.
	
	RUNFOREVER        Do not time out waiting for a client, and do not exit
	                  after the last client has disconnected.
						
	VIEWONLY          Only permit the remote view to view, but not to interact
	                  with the display being served.
						
	SHARED            Allow multiple clients to be connected simultaneously.
	
	FORCE16B          Forces the internal framebuffer to be 16bits depth.
	                  Improves processing speed at the expense of graphic quality.
	
	SCANS             Specify the maximum number of input events or sending 
	                  framebuffer updates at each cycle.
						
	TARGETFPS         Specify target frame per seconds for local frame buffer update search
						
	TW                Specify the width in pixels of the tiles the framebuffer
	                  is divided into.
						
	TH                Specify the height in pixels of the tiles the framebuffer
	                  is divided into.
						
	LOG               Display log information
	
	UNKNOWN_IP        Specify how to react when an unknown IP tries to connect:
	                  - ASK     : Open a requester for the user to answer
	                  - ACCEPT  : Always Accept
	                  - REJECT  : Always Reject Unknown IPs
						
	IPFILTER          Specify IPs that are allowed to connect, several filters can used,
	                  they shall be separated by ";" 
	                  examples : 
	                    192.168.0.1      : this IP is allowed to connect
	                    192.168.0.255    : all IPs starting with 192.168.0. are allowed
	                    255.255.255.255  : Everyone is allowed
						  
	LIBVNCSERVER      Options to be passed to libvncserver
	
The same options can be defined when launched from WB using the icon TOOLTYPES

The user interface can be invoqued using the commodity exchange program.
The five buttons actions are the following:
1. Turn ON/OFF the transmission (usefull to "hide" some actions to the viewers)
2. Hide the GUI
3. Turn ON/OFF logging
4. Settings
5. Shutdown the server

History:
V2.1 (5 August 2026) :
- updated libvncserver to version 0.9.14, now fetched and patched at build
  time instead of being bundled with the sources
- fixed the dirty tile test, which relied on libvncserver's TRUE being -1
  stored in a char array - no framebuffer update was ever sent to a client
  on targets where char is unsigned (riscv64, arm, ppc)
- the screen is always captured as BGRA32, so palette screens are converted
  on capture instead of being sent in the wrong colours
- fixed socket handling on AROS: bsdsocket.library is opened before the
  first call that needs it, errno is wired up through SocketBaseTags(), and
  sockets are released with CloseSocket() rather than close()
- renamed to AROSVNCServer and started from its own package startup script
  once the network stack is online
- reports itself to viewers as "AROSVNCServer"
V2.0 (29 June 2014) :
- Reworked local framebuffer parsing (uses much less memory and is faster)
- updated libvncserver to version 0.9.9
- reworked the GUI
    - non working close gadgets aren't displayed anymore
    - log windows opens at startup when LOG option is selected
V1.5 (24 February 2011) :
- Added FORCE16B option
- Application is now multithreaded, a independent thread is used to collect framebuffer and detect delta
- Added Statistics window with real time tuning of scans and target FPS
- Corrected mouse movements while buttons are pressed
V1.1 (21 February 2011) :
- Improved Delta detection algorithm speed
- Framebuffers are now of same depth as initial screen and no more always 32bits
- Speed improvement in lower depth modes
- Now Active screen is transmitted and not only initial active screen (even if frambuffers remain at initial resolution and depth)

